; 04-multi-agent.scenarios.udon — a day in the life, the whole day
; Concurrency, contention, handoff, and the evening ledger. These are the
; scenarios Joseph asked for by name: "a pretended chronicle of events
; that would be a typical day in the life of multiple agents
; understanding, diffing, and modifying those files." Conventions:
; ../README.md. Path syntax provisional (see 01).

|feature[multi-agent].day-in-the-life
  :date <2026-07-21>
  :tool udon-patch
  :path-syntax provisional-2026-07
  :corpus ../corpus/

  Five agents share the corpus today: surveyor, ordinator, lexicographer
  (twice — see the handoff), and two operators working the same effort.
  No locks, no coordinator process: the discipline is append blocks,
  path-anchored patches, and preconditions.

  |scenario[concurrent-append]
    :at <10:12> :agent operator-1
    :also :at <10:12> :agent operator-2
    :file corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon

    Both operators finish a decision at the same moment. The log's design
    (self-contained top-level blocks, one write each, O_APPEND) is built
    for exactly this.

    |given the decision log as shipped
    |when both agents append-block simultaneously
      |append-block :file corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon :agent operator-1
        !:udon:
          |decision[fixture-mining-order] :date <2026-07-21> :by claude :status proposed :topic testing
            :session 01Aa1bCc2dEe3fGg
            Mine legacy-pre-0.8 fixtures newest-first; recency correlates with surviving semantics.
      |append-block :file corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon :agent operator-2
        !:udon:
          |decision[bench-before-regenerate] :date <2026-07-21> :by claude :status proposed :topic tooling
            :session 01Hh4iJj5kLl6mNn
            Run the criterion suite immediately before AND after every grammar regeneration, as a pair.
    |then both blocks are intact at EOF in either order — never
      interleaved (single write under O_APPEND)
      |expect :decisions 6 :garbled 0
      |invariant re-parsing the whole file yields six top-level
        |decision elements and the Document-layer duplicate-key check
        (default policy: error) passes — slugs were distinct
      The convention the log's header documents is load-bearing: an
      agent can append WITHOUT reading, because column-0 blocks close
      everything by construction. The parser's EOF-as-universal-closer
      ruling is what makes the last block always well-formed.

  |scenario[contested-claim]
    :at <10:30> :agent operator-1
    :also :agent operator-2
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    Both operators want intent 311. The claim patch carries its own
    guard; etiquette is not the mechanism.

    |given the workspace as shipped, where intent 311 has :claimed-by nil
    |when both agents submit the atomic claim patch (require nil, then
      set claimed-by / claimed-at / status) within the same second
    |then exactly one patch applies; the other fails its |require and
      touches nothing
      |expect :winners 1 :losers 1
      |expect-error :agent-loser :code PreconditionFailed
        :detail "||intent[311]:claimed-by is operator-1, expected nil"
      The loser re-reads, sees 311 taken, and claims 312 instead — the
      recovery is a fresh read plus a fresh patch, not a merge. Lost
      updates are impossible by construction, exactly as
      @decision[claim-requires-nil] ruled.

  |scenario[disjoint-subtrees-compose]
    :at <11:58> :agent lexicographer
    :also :agent ordinator
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    Two agents edit ONE file: the lexicographer settles the
    regime-change term (section 1) while the ordinator extends the
    nomos term's :forms (section 2). The lexicographer lands first,
    shifting every byte offset below section 1.

    |given the lexicon as shipped
    |when the lexicographer's patch applies, then the ordinator's patch
      — prepared against the SAME pre-state — is submitted
    |then the ordinator's patch still applies cleanly: its anchors are
      paths, and "||term[nomos]:forms" resolves identically in the
      shifted file
      |expect :applied both :conflicts 0
      |invariant a span- or line-anchored patch prepared against the
        same pre-state would have mis-landed or spuriously conflicted;
        path anchoring is what makes patches COMPOSE
      Staleness is still checked — but at the node grain (the require
      mechanism, or a node-content fingerprint), not the file grain. A
      whole-file base hash would serialize all concurrency through
      one writer; these two edits are honestly independent.

  |scenario[handoff-mid-edit]
    :at <14:30> :agent lexicographer
    :handoff-to lexicographer-2
    :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon

    The lexicographer's context ends mid-restructure of section 2 —
    intent 402, three ops into a five-op plan. What survives a death is
    what was written down: the patch plan is a durable UDON artifact,
    not conversation state.

    |given a patch-plan file the lexicographer maintained as it worked
      (the split: section 2 becomes "runtime" and a new "fidelity"):
      !:udon:
        |patch-plan[lexicon-section-split]
          :file corpus/vivarium.lexicon.udon
          :intent "||intent[402]" ; in operata-live.workspace.udon
          :prepared <2026-07-21T13:58>
          |op[1].applied
            |insert :parent "|lexicon[vivarium]" :position last
              !:udon:
                |section[fidelity] :num 3
                  :name Fidelity & memoization
          |op[2].applied
            |set :path "||section[runtime-fidelity]:name" :value Runtime
          |op[3].applied
            |move :path "||term[fidelity-ladder]" :to "||section[fidelity]"
          |op[4]
            |move :path "||term[rung]" :to "||section[fidelity]"
          |op[5]
            |move :path "||term[memo]" :to "||section[fidelity]"
    |when lexicographer-2 wakes with no shared context, reads the plan,
      and validates it against the CURRENT file before resuming
    |then validation distinguishes three states per op: applied (its
      postcondition already holds), pending (its paths resolve against
      the current tree), and stranded (its paths no longer resolve)
      |expect :ops [applied applied applied pending pending]
      |expect :resume-from 4
      The .applied traits are the outgoing agent's claim; the incoming
      agent VERIFIES rather than trusts — op postconditions make the
      plan re-entrant, so a plan interrupted between write and
      annotation still resumes correctly. This is the affordance the
      packet cannot see from the syntax side: ops need to be stateable
      as predicates, not just executable as commands.

  |scenario[reader-during-writer]
    :at <15:00> :agent steward
    :also :agent operator-1
    :file corpus/operata-live.workspace.udon

    The steward skeleton-reads the workspace for the evening report
    while operator-1's realization patch is mid-flight.

    |given operator-1 writing via the patch tool's write discipline
      (write to temp, fsync, rename over the original)
    |when the steward reads at any instant
    |then the steward sees the pre-patch file or the post-patch file,
      never a torn intermediate
      |expect :torn-reads 0
      |invariant the patch tool never writes a document in place; the
        atomic-rename discipline is part of the tool's contract, same
        standing as computed indentation — without it, every reader
        needs a lock, and the whole multi-agent posture collapses

  |scenario[evening-ledger]
    :at <17:30> :agent steward
    :file corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon

    The steward closes the day: verify the corpus's cross-references
    still hold after eleven writes, then append the day's summary.

    |given the corpus as it stands at 17:30
    |when
      |all :path "||*:feeds@" :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon
      |all :path "||*:fed-by@" :file corpus/asf-processes.process-map.udon
    |then every reference in the signal graph resolves to a definition —
      the cartographer's rename at 14:40 cascaded correctly
      |expect :dangling 0
      (Resolve-audit as a one-liner: `all` over a wildcard element
      segment, a named attribute, and a follow. The steward would have
      liked "every reference-valued attribute, whatever its name" —
      `||*:*@` — which needs the wildcard ATTR segment `:*` to compose
      with `@`-follow; worth confirming that composition is intended.)
    |when the steward appends the summary decision
      |append-block :file corpus/vivarium.decision-log.udon :agent steward
        !:udon:
          |decision[day-2026-07-21-summary] :date <2026-07-21> :by claude :status proposed :topic chronicle
            :session 01Ss7tUu8vWw9xYy
            Eleven writes across five files by five agents; zero lost updates, one contested claim resolved by precondition, one mid-edit handoff resumed from a durable plan.
            |ref test/scenarios/features/
    |then the chronicle is itself in the corpus, and tomorrow's surveyor
      will read it the way today's read yesterday's
      |expect :decisions 7
